export default {"id":"launchkey-level-12","number":12,"title":"External Connectivity & Signal Distribution","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"The Launchkey MK4 61 provides a dedicated 6.35 mm (1/4\" TS) rear-panel jack for foot control. This module covers pedal hardware electrical requirements, default MIDI CC 64 generation, advanced message assignment via Novation Components, and","modules":[{"id":"launchkey-m-12-1-1","code":"12.1","title":"External Connectivity & Signal Distribution","blurb":"The Launchkey MK4 61 provides a dedicated 6.35 mm (1/4\" TS) rear-panel jack for foot control. This module covers pedal hardware electrical requirements, default MIDI CC 64 generation, advanced message assignment via Novation Components, and","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-35","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 12.1 — Sustain & Foot Control Subsystem","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 61 provides a dedicated 6.35 mm (1/4\" TS) rear-panel jack for foot control. This module covers pedal hardware electrical requirements, default MIDI CC 64 generation, advanced message assignment via Novation Components, and the strict physical exclusion of continuous expression and multi-pedal assemblies."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Itemize compatible physical foot switches for the 6.35 mm rear connector.","Define the default MIDI Continuous Controller generated by foot depression (<strong>CC 64</strong>).","Reassign pedal message types (Note, CC, NRPN) within Novation Components.","Formulate the technical rationale why continuous expression pedals and half-damper systems are unsupported."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-1-1","title":"12.1.1: Supported Foot Switch Hardware","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.1: Supported Foot Switch Hardware","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear-panel <code>Sustain</code> jack is designed for binary momentary contact closures:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Supported Hardware</strong>:","Standard Momentary Sustain Pedals (normally-open or normally-closed polarity).","Standard Keyboard Foot Switches (TS 1/4\" mono jack).","<strong>Auto-Polarity Detection</strong>: The Launchkey microcontroller detects pedal polarity on power-up. Connect the pedal before plugging in the USB-C cable to ensure correct open/closed calibration."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-1-2","title":"12.1.2: Standard MIDI Message Structure (CC 64)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.2: Standard MIDI Message Structure (CC 64)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Default Message</strong>: Transmits standard <strong>MIDI CC 64</strong> (Damper / Sustain Pedal).","<strong>Data Values</strong>:","<em>Pedal Depressed</em>: Generates <code>Control Change 64</code>, Value = <strong>127</strong>.","<em>Pedal Released</em>: Generates <code>Control Change 64</code>, Value = <strong>0</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-1-3","title":"12.1.3: Custom Foot Switch Programming in Components","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.3: Custom Foot Switch Programming in Components","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In the Novation Components <code>Pedal</code> tab:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Message Types</strong>: The pedal input can be reassigned to output:","Any standard <strong>MIDI CC</strong> (0–127).","<strong>NRPN</strong> messages (7-bit or 14-bit).","<strong>MIDI Note On/Off</strong> (allowing the foot pedal to trigger a kick drum or vocal cue).","<strong>Behavior</strong>: Programmable as <strong>Momentary</strong> (value 127 while pressed, 0 when released) or <strong>Toggle</strong> (alternates 127 and 0 per press)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-1-4","title":"12.1.4: Strict Hardware Exclusions","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.4: Strict Hardware Exclusions","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Attempting to connect unsupported foot controllers can cause erratic MIDI streaming or electrical malfunction:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    FOOT CONTROLLER COMPATIBILITY TABLE                        |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------+---------------------------+\n| Controller Type                   | Compatibility | Technical Failure Reason  |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------+---------------------------+\n| **Standard Sustain Pedal**        | **COMPATIBLE**| Supported binary switch   |\n| **Momentary Foot Switch**         | **COMPATIBLE**| Supported binary switch   |\n| **Continuous Expression Pedal**   | **EXCLUDED**  | Jack lacks TRS analog     |\n|                                   |               | potentiometer sensing ADC |\n| **Half-Damper Continuous Pedal**  | **EXCLUDED**  | Controller does not read  |\n|                                   |               | intermediate resistance   |\n| **Dual/Triple Piano Assemblies**  | **EXCLUDED**  | Soft / Sostenuto wiring   |\n|                                   |               | unsupported on single TS  |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------+---------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Compatible momentary sustain switches; CC 64 default; Components Pedal tab re-assignment; strict exclusion of expression pedals, half-damper pedals, and triple-pedal units.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Speculation regarding continuous expression adapter hacks."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: Connect a standard sustain pedal to the rear jack. Open a MIDI monitor in your DAW, press and release the pedal, and verify the generation of CC 64 values 127 and 0."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["MIDI monitor records <code>B0 40 7F</code> (CC 64 = 127) upon press and <code>B0 40 00</code> (CC 64 = 0) upon release.","Absence of continuous intermediate values confirmed."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-12-2-2","code":"12.2","title":"External Connectivity & Signal Distribution","blurb":"The single physical USB-C connection of the Launchkey MK4 61 encapsulates two entirely independent logical USB MIDI interfaces. Understanding the strict boundary between Interface 1 (Performing Surface) and Interface 2 (DAW Control Surface)","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-36","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 12.2 — Dual USB MIDI Physical and Logical Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The single physical USB-C connection of the Launchkey MK4 61 encapsulates two entirely independent logical USB MIDI interfaces. Understanding the strict boundary between Interface 1 (Performing Surface) and Interface 2 (DAW Control Surface) is critical for configuring DAW preferences, preventing MIDI feedback loops, and avoiding data corruption."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Formulate the architectural difference between USB Interface 1 (Performing MIDI) and USB Interface 2 (DAW Control Surface).","Identify the exact port nomenclature on Windows and macOS workstations.","Define the MIDI Note-Off transmission convention (<code>Note On with Velocity 0</code>).","Prevent port contention and cross-routing errors in multi-track DAW configurations."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-2-1","title":"12.2.1: Logical Interface Segregation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.2.1: Logical Interface Segregation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Although a single USB-C cable connects the Launchkey MK4 61 to the host computer, the USB endpoint descriptor exposes two distinct logical MIDI devices:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  PHYSICAL USB-C CONNECTION (LAUNCHKEY MK4 61)\n                                      │\n              ┌───────────────────────┴───────────────────────┐\n              ▼                                               ▼\n+-----------------------------+               +-----------------------------+\n|    LOGICAL INTERFACE 1      |               |     LOGICAL INTERFACE 2     |\n|   (PERFORMING SURFACE)      |               |    (DAW CONTROL SURFACE)    |\n+-----------------------------+               +-----------------------------+\n| - 61-Key Semi-Weighted Keys |               | - Bi-directional DAW Script |\n| - Pitch &amp; Modulation Wheels |               | - Session Clip Launch Matrix|\n| - Custom Pad Modes (1–4)    |               | - Plugin Device Automation  |\n| - Custom Encoder Modes (1–4)|               | - Mixer Volume / Pan Meters |\n| - Custom Fader Modes (1–4)  |               | - DAW Transport Handshakes  |\n| - Drum Pad Mode (Ch 10)     |               | - OLED Track Name Sysex     |\n| - Chord Engines (Ch 3)      |               | - DAW Global Scale Sync     |\n+-----------------------------+               +-----------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-2-2","title":"12.2.2: Host Operating System Port Nomenclature","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.2.2: Host Operating System Port Nomenclature","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    DUAL INTERFACE OS NOMENCLATURE MATRIX                      |\n+---------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Interface     | Windows (Novation Driver)     | macOS (Native CoreMIDI)       |\n+---------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Interface 1**| **Launchkey MK4 61 MIDI**     | **Launchkey MK4 61 MIDI**     |\n| (Performance) | *(or LKMK4 MIDI)*             |                               |\n+---------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Interface 2**| **MIDIIN2 (LKMK4 MIDI)** /    | **Launchkey MK4 61 DAW In**   |\n| (DAW Control) | **MIDIOUT2 (LKMK4 MIDI)**     | **Launchkey MK4 61 DAW Out**  |\n|               | *(or Launchkey MK4 Port 2)*   |                               |\n+---------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Mandatory DAW Rule</em>: The DAW Control Surface script (e.g. Ableton Live's <code>Launchkey MK4</code> script) must <strong>only</strong> be assigned to <strong>Interface 2 (DAW In/Out)</strong>. Virtual instrument recording tracks must <strong>only</strong> listen to <strong>Interface 1 (MIDI)</strong>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-2-3","title":"12.2.3: MIDI Note-Off Protocol Convention","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.2.3: MIDI Note-Off Protocol Convention","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 61 utilizes the industry-standard running status optimization for note termination:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Transmission</strong>: Releasing a keybed note or drum pad generates a <strong><code>Note On</code> message with Velocity = 0</strong> (<code>0x90</code>, Note Number, <code>0x00</code>).","<strong>Reception</strong>: When receiving external MIDI to illuminate pads, the internal processor accepts both standard <code>Note Off</code> (<code>0x80</code>, Note Number, Velocity) and <code>Note On with Velocity 0</code> (<code>0x90</code>, Note Number, <code>0x00</code>)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Dual logical interfaces; Interface 1 (performing MIDI) vs Interface 2 (DAW script); Windows vs Mac port strings; Note On with Velocity 0 convention; driverless Windows exclusivity rule.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Speculation regarding undocumented 3rd or 4th USB interfaces."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: In your operating system's MIDI settings or DAW preferences, locate both Interface 1 and Interface 2. Record the exact strings shown on your screen."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Interface 1 identified as performing stream (routed to tracks).","Interface 2 identified as DAW script stream (isolated from direct musical recording)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-12-3-3","code":"12.3","title":"External Connectivity & Signal Distribution","blurb":"The rear-panel 5-pin DIN output enables the Launchkey MK4 61 to control external hardware synthesizers, drum machines, modular rigs, and legacy sound modules without requiring a computer. This module covers hardware DIN signal routing, inte","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-37","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 12.3 — 5-Pin DIN MIDI Output Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The rear-panel 5-pin DIN output enables the Launchkey MK4 61 to control external hardware synthesizers, drum machines, modular rigs, and legacy sound modules without requiring a computer. This module covers hardware DIN signal routing, internal clock broadcasting, channel matching, and physical cabling standards."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Detail the signal routing pipeline from the physical playing surface to the 5-pin DIN MIDI Out connector.","Formulate the clock distribution protocol governed by the <code>MIDI Clock Out</code> setting.","Configure multi-timbral channel matching for standalone hardware synthesizers.","Verify the absence of a 5-pin DIN MIDI In port and formulate the unidirectional nature of the hardware link.","Complete Assessment Gate I."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-3-1","title":"12.3.1: 5-Pin DIN Output Signal Mirroring","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.3.1: 5-Pin DIN Output Signal Mirroring","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear-panel 5-pin DIN connector operates as a dedicated hardware mirror of <strong>USB Interface 1 (MIDI In)</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  HARDWARE DIN SIGNAL ROUTING\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+\n| [ Physical Keys, Wheels, Custom Encoders, Drum Pads ]     |\n|                              │                            |\n|                              ▼                            |\n|                     Onboard Microcontroller               |\n|                              │                            |\n|              ┌───────────────┴───────────────┐            |\n|              ▼                               ▼            |\n|       [ USB Interface 1 ]             [ 5-Pin DIN Out ]   |\n|       (To Host Computer)              (To External Synth) |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Direct Controller Output</strong>: Every keybed stroke, wheel deflection, custom encoder twist, or drum pad hit is instantly converted to a standard 31.25 kBaud opto-isolated current loop signal on the DIN Out.","<strong>Host Reply Isolation</strong>: DAW feedback data arriving from the computer via USB <strong>is not echoed</strong> out of the 5-pin DIN port."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-3-2","title":"12.3.2: Real-time Hardware Clock Distribution","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.3.2: Real-time Hardware Clock Distribution","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Settings Parameter</strong>: <code>MIDI Clock Out</code> (On / Off — Factory Default: <strong>On</strong>).","<strong>Operation</strong>: When the internal arpeggiator is running, the Launchkey broadcasts real-time MIDI Clock (<code>0xF8</code>), MIDI Start (<code>0xFA</code>), and MIDI Stop (<code>0xFC</code>) out of the 5-pin DIN port.","<strong>Synchronizing External Sequencers</strong>: Connecting the DIN Out to an external hardware drum machine or groovebox automatically synchronizes the groovebox tempo to the Launchkey's internal arpeggiator tempo."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-3-3","title":"12.3.3: Multi-Timbral Hardware Channel Matching","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.3.3: Multi-Timbral Hardware Channel Matching","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When controlling external desktop synths without a DAW:"},{"type":"list","items":["Match the external synthesizer's receive channel to the Launchkey's active transmitting channel:","<strong>Synth A (Lead)</strong>: Set synth to Receive Channel 1 -&gt; Played by Keybed Part A.","<strong>Synth B (Bass)</strong>: Set synth to Receive Channel 2 -&gt; Played by Keybed Part B / Split Zone.","<strong>Synth C (Poly Pad)</strong>: Set synth to Receive Channel 3 -&gt; Played by Chord Map pads.","<strong>Drum Machine</strong>: Set unit to Receive Channel 10 -&gt; Played by 16 Drum Pads."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-12-3-4","title":"12.3.4: Cabling & Unidirectional Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.3.4: Cabling & Unidirectional Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Physical Standard</strong>: Standard 5-pin DIN male-to-male shielded MIDI cable (Pins 2, 4, 5 active).","<strong>Unidirectional Constraint</strong>: The Launchkey MK4 61 contains <strong>zero MIDI In circuitry</strong>. It cannot receive MIDI clock, program changes, or note streams from external hardware devices via DIN."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: DIN mirrors USB MIDI In (Interface 1); MIDI Clock Out; multi-timbral synth channel matching; 5-pin DIN cable standard; explicit confirmation of zero MIDI In on the 61.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Invention of DIN MIDI In or MIDI Thru ports."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Assessment Gate I (Connectivity & Ports Validation)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective</strong>: Demonstrate complete operational mastery of the dual USB logical interfaces, 5-pin DIN output, and pedal connectivity."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Self-Assessment Checklist</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Identify both USB MIDI interfaces on your host computer by their exact system strings (e.g., <code>Launchkey MK4 61 MIDI</code> and <code>MIDIIN2 (LKMK4 MIDI)</code> / <code>DAW In</code>).","[ ] Explain why the DAW Control Surface script must only attach to Interface 2 (DAW ports).","[ ] State the exact signal flow of the rear 5-pin DIN output and confirm whether incoming host DAW MIDI is echoed to DIN.","[ ] Define the compatible and excluded foot controllers for the 6.35 mm pedal jack.","[ ] Confirm the Launchkey does not possess a 5-pin MIDI In jack."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
